WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.630
The reigning belief was that the molecules of life carried a vital force that you

2
00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:11.970
could not build them from ordinary, lifeless matter, only borrow them from something

3
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:12.970
already alive.

4
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:20.080
One afternoon in 1828 Wöhler heated an inorganic salt and watched urea crystallize

5
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.380
in his flask. Urea is the nitrogen waste animals flush out through their kidneys.

6
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.480
There was no kidney in the room.

7
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:34.143
He wrote to his mentor Berzelius with a joke, not a manifesto: "I can no longer, as

8
00:00:34.143 --> 00:00:38.603
it were, hold back my chemical urine; and I have to let out that I can make urea

9
00:00:38.603 --> 00:00:39.803
without needing a kidney."

10
00:00:40.543 --> 00:00:43.263
The line is irreverent and exact at once.

11
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:48.033
He was sharing surprise with a friend, and in the same breath dissolving the

12
00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:49.893
boundary the whole century had assumed.

13
00:00:50.433 --> 00:00:53.333
The molecules of life obey the chemistry of everything else.

14
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:59.313
What he had opened he called "a primeval forest, a monstrous and boundless thicket,"

15
00:00:59.773 --> 00:01:02.093
and claimed only the willingness to go in again.

16
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:07.173
The four classes of biomolecules in this chapter are the trees in that forest.
